  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 134 PM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nFriday through Wednesday:\n\nHigh pressure will move across the Upper Great Lakes Region\nthrough Friday, keeping conditions dry and mild. As the high\nexits eastward Friday night, strong WAA over the plains is\nexpected to kick off a thunderstorm complex Friday night into\nSaturday morning. This complex may then ride the nose of the WAA\ninto central and southern Wisconsin Saturday afternoon and\nevening, though some of the CAMs sharply weak these storms as\nthe travel southeast into the core of the remaining sfc ridge.\n\nBetter chances (~60%) for thunderstorms then emerge Saturday \nnight into Sunday as the core of the LLJ that was over the \nplains Friday night moves over the Upper Mississippi Valley. WAA\ndriven thunderstorms are progged to form early Sunday and then\nlinger during the first half of the day, generally weakening as\nthe LLJ wanes. Additional thunderstorms could form during the\nlate afternoon into the evening, though that would be dependent\nupon skies clearing promptly after the morning rain. Long range\nmodels get a bit murkier heading into Monday and Tuesday of next\nweek, but it does look like thunderstorm chances would continue\nas a frontal boundary stalls over the state ahead of an\napproaching low pressure system. With the prolonged WAA and\nstalled frontal boundary, hot and humid weather is expected to\nreturn Sunday into next week, with dew points near 70 and highs\nin the upper 80s.\n\nCMiller",
